1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,060
All right. So, yeah, we're here to talk about burp performance and the recent sort of improvement drive that we've been doing on performance in burp.

2
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:10,220
So here we go.

3
00:00:11,060 --> 00:00:16,180
So as we all know, performance has been a hot topic in the burp community for quite a while now.

4
00:00:16,560 --> 00:00:23,020
People have expressed frustration with large projects and less powerful equipment having trouble running burp.

5
00:00:23,160 --> 00:00:29,900
And in order to address this, we now have the performance squad focused on making burp lag-free and fun to use.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:40,080
So we have been working on improving burp's performance while measuring progress and trying to prevent future regressions.

7
00:00:40,660 --> 00:00:47,060
In order to do this, we've made regression tests and Grafana dashboards to monitor the state of burp performance.

8
00:00:47,500 --> 00:00:54,800
We've also been adding some telemetry and collecting data to help us identify potential performance bottlenecks to target next.

9
00:00:54,800 --> 00:01:07,140
And we also want to make sure that there are visual indications when longer running tasks are happening so that users can know that burp hasn't just given up and frozen.

10
00:01:07,860 --> 00:01:15,380
So we've already made many improvements, such as sitemap filtering speed and lazy extension loading for performance.

11
00:01:15,380 --> 00:01:20,340
So table sorting was the first one we looked at.

12
00:01:21,740 --> 00:01:25,280
It was one of the first performance challenges we took on.

13
00:01:25,740 --> 00:01:31,980
So in some very large proxy history tables, some columns could take up to minutes to sort.

14
00:01:31,980 --> 00:01:39,680
And it was made worse by the fact that all of the sorting happened on the swing thread, locking the UI for the entire duration of the sort.

15
00:01:41,380 --> 00:01:51,180
And this was compounded by having the ability to sort by multiple columns at once, meaning that there could be up to three times as many data retrievals as there were for a single column.

16
00:01:51,180 --> 00:01:58,180
So the first and potentially most obvious thing to address was the speed of the sorting itself.

17
00:01:58,180 --> 00:02:06,180
Java's built-in sorting algorithm is pretty well optimized, so instead of looking at that, we address the more problematic speed of data retrieval.

18
00:02:06,180 --> 00:02:11,180
Often the same piece of data would have to be retrieved multiple times in order to complete a sort.

19
00:02:11,180 --> 00:02:15,980
When this is happening from a project file, this can be very slow.

20
00:02:15,980 --> 00:02:22,180
Pre-loading a temporary cache of the data into memory led to much faster access and overall sort durations.

21
00:02:23,380 --> 00:02:29,920
Even though we were able to make the sort operation faster, we still don't want to lock the UI for its duration.

22
00:02:30,720 --> 00:02:39,100
So in order to do this, we had to figure out a way to move the most intensive tasks off the UI thread, which is quite the challenge when you're trying to preserve data integrity on a table.

23
00:02:39,100 --> 00:02:48,260
And finally, we want to let users know that sorting is in progress and that burp hasn't just crashed or given up while it is doing the processing.

24
00:02:48,260 --> 00:02:51,580
So we had to come up with something for that.

25
00:02:51,580 --> 00:02:53,700
So on to the actual solution.

26
00:02:53,700 --> 00:03:02,400
We created a custom implementation of a row sorter, which takes a snapshot of the data to be sorted and pulls it into memory.

27
00:03:02,400 --> 00:03:12,200
We then sort it and apply the calculated indices to the table view and use read-write locking to control access to the table.

28
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:19,000
The write operations are queued to apply in a batch after the sorting is completed.

29
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:31,200
And to ensure the reliability of these changes, we wrote quite a suite of like multi-threaded stress tests and specific event orderings to test edge cases.

30
00:03:31,200 --> 00:03:38,200
Finally, we also surfaced the actual sorting operation in the UI using spinners in the column headers.

31
00:03:38,200 --> 00:03:44,800
So this is a regression test plot of the sorting test.

32
00:03:44,800 --> 00:03:50,200
We're sorting by multiple fields in a very large proxy history file, like hundreds of thousands of items.

33
00:03:50,200 --> 00:03:56,800
And you can see there's a precipitous drop in the amount of duration it took down to like two seconds or less.

34
00:03:56,800 --> 00:04:00,800
So on to repeater.

35
00:04:00,800 --> 00:04:02,800
I'm going to pass over to my colleague, Jay.

36
00:04:02,800 --> 00:04:03,800
Hey guys.

37
00:04:03,800 --> 00:04:04,800
Okay.

38
00:04:04,800 --> 00:04:09,800
So within repeater tool, you're able to make your own HTTP requests.

39
00:04:09,800 --> 00:04:14,800
And this is probably the place within Burp a lot of pen testers are going to sink their hours into.

40
00:04:14,800 --> 00:04:17,800
You can also send data to the repeater from other tools.

41
00:04:17,800 --> 00:04:22,800
And a problem a lot of people run into is just like browser tags, tabs,

42
00:04:22,800 --> 00:04:27,800
because a lot of these can build up over time.

43
00:04:27,800 --> 00:04:33,800
Organizer was released last year as a way to help people to keep their workflows a bit less chaotic.

44
00:04:33,800 --> 00:04:37,800
But people still have a lot of repeater tabs sitting around.

45
00:04:37,800 --> 00:04:42,800
Unfortunately, we weren't dealing with these in the best way.

46
00:04:42,800 --> 00:04:49,800
Repeater contains lots of UI elements, fundamentally the request view, the response view and the inspector.

47
00:04:49,800 --> 00:04:54,800
Can you hear okay?

48
00:04:54,800 --> 00:04:59,800
Yeah, I can hear.

49
00:04:59,800 --> 00:05:00,800
Cool, thank you.

50
00:05:00,800 --> 00:05:02,800
Each of these components are quite heavy weight.

51
00:05:02,800 --> 00:05:05,800
The message editors have rich editors supporting syntax highlighting,

52
00:05:05,800 --> 00:05:09,800
and the inspector contains a number of nested table and text area components.

53
00:05:09,800 --> 00:05:16,800
Up to now, repeater tabs have been implemented by creating one set of these UI components per tab.

54
00:05:16,800 --> 00:05:21,800
This makes a lot of sense conceptually, but as a drawback that it can eat up a lot of memory per tab.

55
00:05:21,800 --> 00:05:25,800
We could attempt to optimise memory used by the UI, but the problem still remains.

56
00:05:25,800 --> 00:05:28,800
The more tabs you have, the worse performance gets.

57
00:05:28,800 --> 00:05:32,800
Even if you aren't using repeater at the moment, if your project file has repeater data,

58
00:05:32,800 --> 00:05:35,800
then it will always be eating memory in the background.

59
00:05:35,800 --> 00:05:37,800
This has led to the single repeater work.

60
00:05:37,800 --> 00:05:42,800
So the main goal is to retain the existing behaviour, but do it in a much more memory efficient way.

61
00:05:42,800 --> 00:05:46,800
In this case, by only using one set of UI components.

62
00:05:46,800 --> 00:05:51,800
Before delving into the code, we spent a lot of time planning out how we were actually going to measure

63
00:05:51,800 --> 00:05:53,800
if we had achieved our objectives.

64
00:05:53,800 --> 00:05:56,800
We've done a lot of work previously with creating performance tests.

65
00:05:56,800 --> 00:06:01,800
These are our automated processes where robots control and stress test burp,

66
00:06:01,800 --> 00:06:05,800
to ensure that we catch any cases where we introduce performance regression,

67
00:06:05,800 --> 00:06:08,800
including testing both memory usage and time taken.

68
00:06:08,800 --> 00:06:14,800
We started by expanding our coverage for repeater, attempting to benchmark a lot of common scenarios,

69
00:06:14,800 --> 00:06:19,800
such as multi-megabyte JavaScript responses and operations involving group tests.

70
00:06:19,800 --> 00:06:26,800
We then started to plot our regression tests onto graphs so we could see over time easily

71
00:06:26,800 --> 00:06:29,800
if we were accidentally introducing performance issues into burp.

72
00:06:29,800 --> 00:06:34,800
With our regression test data, we could then start to carry out the work.

73
00:06:34,800 --> 00:06:37,800
We had a very simple problem with our approach.

74
00:06:37,800 --> 00:06:41,800
If we're making everything use the same UI component, then everything will have the same content.

75
00:06:41,800 --> 00:06:43,800
That's not ideal.

76
00:06:43,800 --> 00:06:48,800
What's more, you'd also lose any state of any fine-grained settings that you have configured per tab,

77
00:06:48,800 --> 00:06:55,800
such as the line-wrap toggle, or setting to hide uninterested headers.

78
00:06:55,800 --> 00:06:58,800
In fact, there's quite a lot of state we need to look after.

79
00:06:58,800 --> 00:07:03,800
Not just the request response, but things such as edit history, what your search is,

80
00:07:03,800 --> 00:07:07,800
what text you have selected, the scroll position and your view mode.

81
00:07:07,800 --> 00:07:11,800
There are other places in burp which we use the same editor for different items,

82
00:07:11,800 --> 00:07:13,800
such as logger and organiser.

83
00:07:13,800 --> 00:07:16,800
However, in those cases you can't edit each item,

84
00:07:16,800 --> 00:07:19,800
so it's a lot less important to have such granular settings.

85
00:07:19,800 --> 00:07:21,800
It wouldn't work well for us.

86
00:07:21,800 --> 00:07:25,800
So we needed a way to hold onto certain bits of state for each tab,

87
00:07:25,800 --> 00:07:27,800
whilst being as memory efficient as we can.

88
00:07:27,800 --> 00:07:30,800
So we introduced the concept of a checkpoint.

89
00:07:30,800 --> 00:07:35,800
Let's say you have some repeater tabs which you've opened before.

90
00:07:35,800 --> 00:07:42,800
If you click onto tab 1, then as we switch to it, we look at a checkpoint which is stored for that tab,

91
00:07:42,800 --> 00:07:45,800
and it's basically a snapshot of the state of the UI for that tab,

92
00:07:45,800 --> 00:07:48,800
stored with as little memory usage as possible.

93
00:07:48,800 --> 00:07:52,800
We tell the UI to restore itself to the state represented by the checkpoint,

94
00:07:52,800 --> 00:07:59,800
and that causes a lot of calls into the UI to set properties to get it to how it was before.

95
00:07:59,800 --> 00:08:02,800
Now let's say I make some modifications within tab 1,

96
00:08:02,800 --> 00:08:05,800
and then want to switch to tab 2.

97
00:08:05,800 --> 00:08:09,800
As soon as I click switch, we first capture our state into a checkpoint,

98
00:08:09,800 --> 00:08:12,800
and replace our old checkpoint with this new one.

99
00:08:12,800 --> 00:08:15,800
Then we restore the checkpoint for tab 2 on top of the current UI.

100
00:08:15,800 --> 00:08:20,800
And if we've done everything correctly, everything is looking exactly as it should.

101
00:08:20,800 --> 00:08:23,800
In reality, this process should be almost instant.

102
00:08:23,800 --> 00:08:26,800
And then finally, if we create a new tab,

103
00:08:26,800 --> 00:08:30,800
we create a checkpoint of tab 2 to start with,

104
00:08:30,800 --> 00:08:33,800
then run an initialization step for the UI,

105
00:08:33,800 --> 00:08:36,800
which will restore it to a default state.

106
00:08:36,800 --> 00:08:40,800
That way no current tab data leaks across into this new empty tab.

107
00:08:40,800 --> 00:08:45,800
Each tab contains a much more lightweight representation of its content.

108
00:08:45,800 --> 00:08:56,800
The UI doesn't hold any non-UI related state,

109
00:08:56,800 --> 00:09:02,800
and tabs are lazily initialized and only populated when clicked on.

110
00:09:02,800 --> 00:09:06,800
So, the outcomes of the work were positive.

111
00:09:06,800 --> 00:09:09,800
The changes were able to quite dramatically decrease

112
00:09:09,800 --> 00:09:12,800
some time taken and memory measurement measurements.

113
00:09:12,800 --> 00:09:14,800
We've also updated our red regression threshold line,

114
00:09:14,800 --> 00:09:18,800
which means our builds fail if we lose any performance gains we've made.

115
00:09:18,800 --> 00:09:20,800
We posted one of the graphs on our Twitter,

116
00:09:20,800 --> 00:09:23,800
which got some good engagement, which was nice to see.

117
00:09:23,800 --> 00:09:31,800
We also saw a downward trend with a lot of other performance tests too.

118
00:09:31,800 --> 00:09:33,800
So, what do the actual changes look like in reality?

119
00:09:33,800 --> 00:09:44,800
In this scenario, we're loading up Perp with the same project file,

120
00:09:44,800 --> 00:09:50,800
which contains 250 repeated tabs, each holding a GET request to the JIN and JUICE homepage.

121
00:09:50,800 --> 00:09:55,800
We're able to save 10 seconds on the project load and decrease the memory usage tenfold,

122
00:09:55,800 --> 00:10:01,800
down from 1.5 GB to around 130 MB.

123
00:10:01,800 --> 00:10:02,800
We've got a lot of data.

124
00:10:02,800 --> 00:10:09,800
Right, in this next scenario, we've got a tab group with 250 tabs,

125
00:10:09,800 --> 00:10:12,800
all with the same request to the JIN and JUICE homepage.

126
00:10:12,800 --> 00:10:15,800
The requests are issued on a single connection for all the tabs,

127
00:10:15,800 --> 00:10:19,800
and you'll notice a speed improvement for how long it takes until the request group is done.

128
00:10:19,800 --> 00:10:22,800
This is because it needs to do much less UI work,

129
00:10:22,800 --> 00:10:26,800
which impacts how long requests take to be processed outside of the network layer.

130
00:10:26,800 --> 00:10:31,800
You'll notice a dramatically larger difference for requests containing bigger messages as well.

131
00:10:31,800 --> 00:10:38,800
Right, and the final example is a scenario where we're cycling between 100 repeated tabs.

132
00:10:38,800 --> 00:10:43,800
We had this test because the main trade-off of having a single set of UI components

133
00:10:43,800 --> 00:10:46,800
